#doc(title: [Projectiles: worked examples], subject: "Year 12 physics")

#element example(
  title: content = none,
  numbered: bool = true,
  difficulty: choice("core", "extension") = "core",
) {
  kind: block
  body: slots(statement: blocks, solution: blocks)
  counter: within heading(level: 1)
  ref-key: "example"
  words: (en: "Example", pl: "przykł.")
}

#element formula(name: str) {
  kind: block
  body: blocks
}

#element keyword() {
  kind: inline
  body: inline
}

= Projectiles

A projectile moves under gravity alone, so its horizontal velocity is constant and its vertical acceleration is #keyword[$g$ downwards]. Resolve every motion into these two components before you use the equations of motion.

#example(title: [Projectile on a slope], difficulty: "extension")[
  #statement[A particle is projected up a plane inclined at $alpha$ to the horizontal, with speed $u$ at an angle $theta$ to the plane. Find the time of flight.]
  #solution[Resolve along and perpendicular to the plane. Perpendicular to it the acceleration is $g cos alpha$, so the particle lands after $t = (2 u sin theta) / (g cos alpha)$.]
] <ex:slope>

#example(title: [Horizontal launch])[
  #statement[A ball is thrown horizontally at $12 "m/s"$ from the top of a cliff $45 "m"$ high. How far from the foot of the cliff does it land?]
  #solution[It falls $45 "m"$ in $t = sqrt(2 dot 45 / 9.8) = 3.03 "s"$, so it lands $36.4 "m"$ from the foot.]
]

#example(class: "short")[
  #statement[A stone is dropped from rest. How far does it fall in the first two seconds?]
  #solution[$s = 1/2 g t^2 = 19.6 "m"$.]
]

The method of @ex:slope works for any inclined plane.

#formula(name: "Range on level ground")[
  $ R = (u^2 sin 2 theta) / g $
]

#formula(name: "Greatest height")[
  $ H = (u^2 sin^2 theta) / (2 g) $
]
